4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186235372 1071 "${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186235390 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186235390 1071 "$\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186235391 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186235391 1071 "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186235410 1074 "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+3{x}^{2} & =-4{z}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,-2+3{x}^{2}\\le y\\le -4{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=2.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2+3{x}^{2}}^{-4{z}^{2}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4{z}^{2})-(-2+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\n$$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{6}/3}^{\\sqrt{6}/3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}}/2}^{\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}}/2}\\,2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2-3{x}^{2}}{4}+(2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186235410 1074 "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+3{x}^{2} & =-4{z}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,-2+3{x}^{2}\\le y\\le -4{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=2.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2+3{x}^{2}}^{-4{z}^{2}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4{z}^{2})-(-2+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{6}/3}^{\\sqrt{6}/3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}}/2}^{\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}}/2}\\,2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-3{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2-3{x}^{2}}{4}+(2-3{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186235411 1074 "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}^{3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})-(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})+(1-2{x}^{2})+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-8{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186235411 1074 "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}^{3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})-(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})+(1-2{x}^{2})+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-8{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186235414 1071 "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(2)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)-f(2)}{x-2}=-{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x\\,+\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186235414 1071 "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n${f}^{′}(2)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)-f(2)}{x-2}=-{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}} \n$).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186235416 1071 "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,h\\,).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186235416 1071 "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}($\n{{field-1}